'''Scott MacDonald''' {{born|24|October|1959}} is an actor who appeared in the first four live-action ''[[Star Trek]]'' television spin-offs. His most frequent ''Star Trek'' role was that of the [[Xindi-Reptilian]] [[Dolim]] during the [[ENT Season 3|third season]] of {{s|ENT}}.

After his first appearance as [[Tosk]] in {{s|DS9}}{{'}}s {{e|Captive Pursuit}}, MacDonald was promised a less makeup-intensive role in any future ''Trek'' appearances. However, three more characters he played required extensive makeup: that of a [[Romulan]], a [[Jem'Hadar]] and a Xindi-Reptilian. He did get the opportunity to play a [[Human]], [[Rollins]], in the {{s|VOY}} [[VOY Season 1|first season]] episode {{e|Caretaker}}. During this episode, his character was placed in command of the {{USS|Voyager}} while the senior officers were away &ndash; a rarity for a non-regular character in ''Star Trek''.

== Career ==
=== Television ===
MacDonald's 1993 appearances as Tosk and [[N'Vek]] on ''Deep Space Nine'' and {{s|TNG}} respectively, were his first television roles. He has appeared on several other science fiction/fantasy shows since then, including ''Kindred: The Embraced'' (with [[Jeff Kober]] and [[Brian Thompson]]), {{wt|Space: Above and Beyond}} (with [[Lanei Chapman]], [[Marnie McPhail]] and [[James Parks]]), {{wt|Charmed}} (with [[Michael Bailey Smith]]), ''[[The X-Files]]'' (2001, starring [[Gillian Anderson]], with [[Wade Andrew Williams]], [[Jennifer S. Parsons]], [[Dan Desmond]], and [[Kenneth Meseroll]]), and ''[[Stargate SG-1]]''.

According to MacDonald, he learned later that he was meant to return as N'Vek in seven more episodes as part of a Vulcan/Romulan reunification arc, several of which would have included [[Spock]], but a falling-out betweeen [[Rick Berman]] and [[Leonard Nimoy]] resulted in this being cancelled. On his last day of shooting {{TNG|Face Of The Enemy}} MacDonald received the script revisions that killed his character.{{el|player.fm/series/the-center-seat-after-show/episode-10-trek-stars-with-scott-macdonald}}

Some of MacDonald's early non-''Trek'' TV credits included appearances on ''[[Frasier]]'' (1996, starring [[Kelsey Grammer]]), {{wt|NYPD Blue}} (1996, starring [[Gordon Clapp]] and [[Sharon Lawrence]] with [[Michael Harney]]), {{wt|Family Law (American TV series)|Family Law}} (2001, starring [[Christopher McDonald]], [[Salli Elise Richardson]], [[Julie Warner]], [[Michelle Horn]] and [[Orla Brady]], with [[Dion Anderson]], [[Earl Boen]] and [[Tracy Middendorf]]), and {{wt|The Practice}} (with [[Bill Smitrovich]]). He later guest-starred in episodes of {{wt|CSI: Crime Scene Investigation}} (starring [[Paul Guilfoyle]] and [[Wallace Langham]]), {{wt|Crossing Jordan}} (starring [[Miguel Ferrer]], with [[Cliff DeYoung]], [[Clint Howard]], [[David Burke]], [[Callan White]], [[Bahni Turpin]] and [[Dave Power]]), {{wt|NCIS (TV series)|NCIS}} (2002, directed by [[Terrence O'Hara]], with [[Sam Witwer]] and [[Brant Cotton]]), {{wt|The District}} (2003, starring [[Roger Aaron Brown]], with [[Frank Novak]] and [[John Savage]]), {{wt|JAG (TV series)|JAG}} (with [[David Andrews]], [[Scott Lawrence]], [[Richard Lineback]] and [[Zoe McLellan]]), and {{wt|Dexter (TV series)|Dexter}} (with [[Brad William Henke]]).

MacDonald also appeared in an episode of {{wt|The Lyon's Den}} (2003, with ''Voyager'' stars [[Roxann Dawson]] and [[Robert Picardo]], fellow ''Deep Space Nine'' guest star [[Steven Weber]] and ''Enterprise'' guest [[Rick Worthy]]). Three years later, MacDonald was seen on {{wt|Boston Legal}}, starring [[William Shatner]] and [[René Auberjonois]]. He later appeared on {{wt|Without a Trace}} (2009, starring [[Enrique Murciano]]).

=== Recurring roles ===
MacDonald was a recurring actor on the HBO series ''Carnivàle'', on which he played the role of Burley. Among those he worked with on this series were [[Adrienne Barbeau]], [[Clancy Brown]], [[K Callan]], [[John Fleck]], [[Ellen Geer]], [[Toby Huss]], [[Leonard Kelly-Young]], [[Thomas Kopache]], [[John Carroll Lynch]], [[Matt McCoy]], [[Diane Salinger]], John Savage and [[Time Winters]].

After ''Carnivàle'', MacDonald played the recurring role of Captain Manning on {{wt|Threshold (TV series)|Threshold}}. This series starred [[Brent Spiner]] and was produced by [[André Bormanis]] and [[David Livingston]] and executive produced by [[Brannon Braga]]; [[Michael Sussman]] was the show's supervising producer. MacDonald also worked with [[Jacqueline Kim]] on this series and appeared in at least one episode written by Sussman.

=== Films ===
MacDonald has appeared in several films, beginning with 1993's ''Fire in the Sky'' (with [[Noble Willingham]]). In 1996, he played the title role in {{wt|Jack Frost (1998 film)|Jack Frost}} and returned to the role for {{wt|Jack Frost 2: Revenge of the Mutant Killer Snowman}} (2000, starring [[Ian Abercrombie]] and [[Doug Jones]]). In 2005, MacDonald gave a memorable performance as Drill Instructor Fitch in {{wt|Jarhead (film)|Jarhead}}.

His other film credits include ''Bad City Blues'' (1999, with [[Simon Billig]], [[Michael McGrady]] and [[Jim Metzler]]), {{wt|Seven Girlfriends}} (1999, with [[Olivia d'Abo]], [[Michael Buchman Silver]], [[Stephanie Erb]], [[Steve Vinovich]], [[Georgann Johnson]], [[Lisa Lord]] and [[Ivar Brogger]]), and {{wt|Straight into Darkness}} (2005, with [[Linda Thorson]], [[Daniel Roebuck]] and [[David Warner]]). He can also be seen in {{wt|The Rat Pack (film)|The Rat Pack}} (1998, with [[David Andrews]], [[Brad Blaisdell]] and [[Dey Young]]), {{wt|Babylon 5: A Call to Arms}} (1999, with [[Marjean Holden]], [[Tracy Scoggins]] and [[Tony Todd]]) and {{wt|Jarhead (film)|Jarhead}} (2005, with [[Dendrie Taylor]]).

=== Stage ===
MacDonald portrayed Damis in a 1988 production of ''Tartuffe'' for the Seattle Repertory Theatre. {{el|seattlepi.nwsource.com/archives/1988/8801060405.asp}} His other credits with the company include productions of ''Richard III'' (portraying Richmond) and ''The Understanding'' (as Raff). He also did several productions with Seattle's Intiman Theatre, including the lead roles in ''A Streetcar Named Desire'' and ''Hamlet''. {{el|speedreels.com/talent/smacdonald/res_smacdonald.pdf}}

MacDonald played the dual role of Patrick and Scotty in [[Robert Schenkkan]]'s Pulitzer Prize-winning series of one-act plays entitled ''The Kentucky Cycle''. The plays began in Seattle and ultimately transferred to Broadway, where it ran for 15 previews and 33 performances from 14 November 1993 through 12 December that same year. {{el|ibdb.com/production.php?id{{=}}4582}} Among the actors MacDonald performed with on ''The Kentucky Cycle'' since its beginning were [[Gregory Itzin]] and [[Randy Oglesby]].

